1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:05,639
Section 5.7 Dangerous propensity
evaluations we introduced the basics

2
00:00:05,639 --> 00:00:09,359
of propensity evaluations in the section
on evaluated properties.

3
00:00:09,839 --> 00:00:13,080
This section will build upon that overview
and explore

4
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:16,760
specific propensities like power seeking
or deception and see

5
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:20,239
how we might design evaluations around
them. General Process

6
00:00:20,239 --> 00:00:25,039
for Propensity Evaluations we covered some
general evaluation techniques

7
00:00:25,039 --> 00:00:29,280
in the earlier sections. Propensity
evaluations can utilize many

8
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:32,159
of them, like Ben Best of N sampling, to

9
00:00:32,159 --> 00:00:36,880
understand distribution of behavioral
tendencies. So there is overlap,

10
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:39,920
but there are also specific techniques
that only pertain

11
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:45,799
to propensity evaluations. 1. Comparative
choice frameworks Unlike capability

12
00:00:45,799 --> 00:00:50,840
evaluations, which often have clear
success failure criteria, propensity

13
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:54,439
evaluations need to present models with
situations where multiple

14
00:00:54,439 --> 00:00:59,710
valid choices exist, each revealing
different underlying tendencies. These

15
00:00:59,710 --> 00:01:03,070
scenarios must be carefully designed so
that all choices

16
00:01:03,070 --> 00:01:06,670
are within the model's capabilities.
Different choices reflect different

17
00:01:06,670 --> 00:01:10,829
underlying propensities, and the framing
doesn't artificially bias towards

18
00:01:10,829 --> 00:01:17,510
particular choices. 2. Consistency
measurement with Propensities we're
interested

19
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:19,510
not just in what a model can do, but

20
00:01:19,510 --> 00:01:22,870
what it reliably chooses to do across
varied contexts.

21
00:01:23,739 --> 00:01:26,780
This can involve testing the same
underlying choice across

22
00:01:26,780 --> 00:01:31,099
different scenarios. We can vary surface
level details while

23
00:01:31,099 --> 00:01:34,859
maintaining the core decision. This
approach can also utilize

24
00:01:34,859 --> 00:01:38,219
the long term interaction studies
described in our Evaluation

25
00:01:38,299 --> 00:01:42,140
techniques section, but with a specific
focus on behavioral

26
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:47,900
consistency rather than maximum
capability. 3. Trade off scenarios

27
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:51,739
these are situations where different
tendencies conflict, forcing the

28
00:01:51,739 --> 00:01:55,579
model to reveal its underlying priorities.
We might create

29
00:01:55,579 --> 00:01:59,620
scenarios where being maximally helpful
conflicts with being completely

30
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:03,180
honest, where short term obedience
conflicts with long term

31
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:07,019
safety, or where transparency trades off
against being effective.

32
00:02:07,579 --> 00:02:10,620
Designing such scenarios where the model
must choose between

33
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:14,500
even multiple positive behaviors can help
reveal which tendencies

34
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:17,819
the model prioritizes when it can't
satisfy all positive

35
00:02:17,819 --> 00:02:23,780
behaviors simultaneously. Challenges in
Propensity Evaluation Design when
designing

36
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:26,659
propensity evaluations, we have to make
sure that we

37
00:02:26,659 --> 00:02:30,620
are measuring genuine behavioral
tendencies rather than artifacts of

38
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:34,819
our evaluation setup. Modern language
models are highly sensitive

39
00:02:34,819 --> 00:02:38,300
to context and framing, which means subtle
aspects of

40
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:41,580
how we structure our evaluations can
dramatically impact the

41
00:02:41,580 --> 00:02:45,379
results. This kind of sensitivity creates
a serious risk

42
00:02:45,379 --> 00:02:48,770
of measuring what we accidentally prompted
for rather than

43
00:02:48,770 --> 00:02:52,849
true underlying propensities. One easy way
to mitigate this

44
00:02:52,849 --> 00:02:55,569
kind of thing is to just use multiple
complementary

45
00:02:55,569 --> 00:02:59,849
approaches as a default. Besides just
careful evaluation design,

46
00:03:00,009 --> 00:03:03,370
we would ideally use both behavioral and
internal evaluation

47
00:03:03,370 --> 00:03:07,409
techniques described earlier, while
varying the context and framing

48
00:03:07,409 --> 00:03:10,810
of scenarios to look for consistent
patterns. How much

49
00:03:10,810 --> 00:03:16,030
do propensity evaluations matter relative
to capability evaluations? The

50
00:03:16,030 --> 00:03:18,949
question of what to allocate limited
resources to is

51
00:03:18,949 --> 00:03:22,030
important Is it more important to measure
what a

52
00:03:22,030 --> 00:03:24,310
system can do or what it tends to do?

53
00:03:24,710 --> 00:03:29,430
The relative importance of propensity
versus capability evaluations changes

54
00:03:29,430 --> 00:03:33,669
as AIs become more powerful. Capability
evaluations are important

55
00:03:33,669 --> 00:03:36,550
for all levels of systems, including those
not at

56
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:40,870
the frontier. However, as systems approach
thresholds for certain

57
00:03:40,870 --> 00:03:45,800
specific dangerous capabilities,
propensity evaluations will become
increasingly more

58
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:50,520
important. At very high capability levels,
propensity evaluations might

59
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:54,159
be our main tool for preventing
catastrophic outcomes. Some

60
00:03:54,159 --> 00:03:57,800
propensities also might be capability
dependent or rely on

61
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:02,120
other propensities. For example, scheming
requires both the capability

62
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:05,080
and propensity for deception because it
must both be

63
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:08,439
able and inclined to hide its true
objectives. It

64
00:04:08,439 --> 00:04:12,520
also requires abilities like situational
awareness to distinguish between

65
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:16,519
whether it is being trained, evaluated, or
deployed. A

66
00:04:16,519 --> 00:04:19,160
scheming model must also have the
propensity for long

67
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:22,399
term planning because it needs to care
about consequences

68
00:04:22,399 --> 00:04:25,560
of its actions after the training episode
is complete

69
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:28,120
and be able to reason about and optimize
for

70
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:32,839
future consequences. Section Deception
Propensity when we talk about

71
00:04:32,839 --> 00:04:37,199
deceptive propensities in AI systems,
we're actually discussing several

72
00:04:37,199 --> 00:04:40,639
closely related but distinct concepts that
are often confused

73
00:04:40,639 --> 00:04:45,279
or conflated. Understanding these
distinctions is crucial because each

74
00:04:45,279 --> 00:04:48,560
concept represents a different aspect of
how models handle

75
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:52,480
and express information. A model might
excel at honesty

76
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:56,319
while failing at truthfulness, or avoid
explicit deception while

77
00:04:56,319 --> 00:05:00,519
engaging in sycophancy. A model's honesty
propensity refers to

78
00:05:00,519 --> 00:05:04,730
its tendency to faithfully express its
internal states regardless

79
00:05:04,730 --> 00:05:07,569
of whether those states are factually
correct or uncertain.

80
00:05:08,209 --> 00:05:10,930
LLMs are trained to predict what humans
would write

81
00:05:10,930 --> 00:05:13,569
and not what is true. Think about what
happens

82
00:05:13,569 --> 00:05:15,730
when we ask a model, what is the capital

83
00:05:15,730 --> 00:05:20,089
of France? If the model's internal
representations, things like

84
00:05:20,089 --> 00:05:24,449
activation patterns or logit
distributions, show strong certainty
around

85
00:05:24,529 --> 00:05:27,529
Lyon, an honest model would say the
capital of

86
00:05:27,529 --> 00:05:31,769
France is Lyon, even though this is
incorrect. Similarly,

87
00:05:31,769 --> 00:05:35,329
if its internal states show uncertainty
between multiple cities,

88
00:05:35,649 --> 00:05:39,970
an honest model would express this
uncertainty directly. I'm

89
00:05:39,970 --> 00:05:43,170
uncertain, but I think it might be Lyon.
The

90
00:05:43,170 --> 00:05:46,129
key is that honest models maintain
alignment between their

91
00:05:46,129 --> 00:05:49,529
internal states and outputs even when
those states are

92
00:05:49,529 --> 00:05:53,329
wrong. At this point, the textbook shows
Figure 5.43.

93
00:05:53,730 --> 00:05:56,569
It depicts example of an AI black box Lie

94
00:05:56,569 --> 00:06:01,040
detector. Truthfulness represents a
stronger property than honesty because

95
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:06,240
it requires two components working
accurate internal representations and

96
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:10,600
honest expression of those
representations. A truthful model saying

97
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:13,759
the capital of France is Paris must both
internally

98
00:06:13,759 --> 00:06:16,879
represent the correct information that
Paris is the capital

99
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:20,720
and maintain the propensity to faithfully
output this information.

100
00:06:21,839 --> 00:06:24,959
This is why truthfulness is harder to
achieve than

101
00:06:24,959 --> 00:06:29,139
either accuracy or honesty alone. It
requires both properties

102
00:06:29,139 --> 00:06:34,379
simultaneously. Deceptive propensity is
the inverse of honesty. It's

103
00:06:34,379 --> 00:06:38,180
a tendency to intentionally produce
outputs that diverge from

104
00:06:38,180 --> 00:06:42,620
internal states when doing so provides
some advantage. Using

105
00:06:42,620 --> 00:06:46,899
our France example, again, a deceptive
model whose internal

106
00:06:46,899 --> 00:06:51,300
representations point to Lyon might still
output Paris if

107
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:53,699
it has learned that this answer gets
better rewards.

108
00:06:54,519 --> 00:06:57,839
This is true deception because there's an
intentional misalignment

109
00:06:57,839 --> 00:07:02,120
between internal state and output
motivated by advantage, in

110
00:07:02,120 --> 00:07:06,800
this case reward maximization. The
distinction between honest mistakes

111
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:11,240
and deception lies in this intentional
misalignment. An honest

112
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:14,000
model getting things wrong is different
from a model

113
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,839
choosing to misrepresent what it knows.
Sycophants represents a

114
00:07:17,839 --> 00:07:22,139
specific form of deceptive propensity, one
where models tell

115
00:07:22,139 --> 00:07:24,899
users what they want to hear rather than
expressing

116
00:07:24,899 --> 00:07:28,740
their true internal states. While general
deception might be

117
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:34,740
motivated by various advantages, Rewards,
achieving goals, etc. Sycophantic

118
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:38,819
behavior specifically optimizes for user
approval. This makes it

119
00:07:38,819 --> 00:07:43,019
particularly concerning from an alignment
perspective, as models might

120
00:07:43,019 --> 00:07:46,300
learn to hide concerning behaviors or
beliefs simply to

121
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:50,089
maintain user satisfaction. How do
hallucinations fit into this

122
00:07:50,089 --> 00:07:55,449
framework? Understanding the propensity
for generating hallucinations helps
complete

123
00:07:55,449 --> 00:07:58,930
our picture and clear taxonomy of these
interrelated concepts.

124
00:07:59,569 --> 00:08:02,810
While deception is the inverse of honesty,
hallucination can

125
00:08:02,810 --> 00:08:05,970
be viewed as the inverse of truthfulness.
It occurs

126
00:08:05,970 --> 00:08:10,569
when a model accurately conveys faulty
internal representations. This

127
00:08:10,569 --> 00:08:14,180
gives us a full matrix of possibilities. A
model

128
00:08:14,180 --> 00:08:18,379
can be honest but hallucinating,
faithfully, expressing incorrect internal

129
00:08:18,379 --> 00:08:23,100
states. It can be deceptive and
hallucinating, misrepresenting already

130
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:26,779
incorrect states. It can be deceptive but
not hallucinating,

131
00:08:26,860 --> 00:08:30,779
misrepresenting correct states. Or it can
be truthful, correct

132
00:08:30,779 --> 00:08:34,740
states expressed faithfully. There is
figure 5.44 in the

133
00:08:34,740 --> 00:08:40,539
textbook. Here it depicts distinguishing
honesty, truthfulness, hallucination,
deception,

134
00:08:40,539 --> 00:08:44,279
and scheming. If a model is faithfully
outputting its

135
00:08:44,279 --> 00:08:47,759
internal beliefs, then it is honest. If
these beliefs

136
00:08:47,759 --> 00:08:50,919
correspond to reality, then it is factual,
else it

137
00:08:50,919 --> 00:08:54,120
is hallucinating. A model might just say
things that

138
00:08:54,120 --> 00:08:56,960
help it get high reward as a myopic
strategy

139
00:08:56,960 --> 00:09:00,240
with no ulterior long term motive. This
can be

140
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:03,480
akin to sycophancy. A model which is
deceptive but

141
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:07,970
with situationally aware long term plans
is scheming deceptively.

142
00:09:08,690 --> 00:09:12,850
Why is measuring deceptive propensities so
challenging for future

143
00:09:12,850 --> 00:09:17,570
powerful AIs? The core challenge in
evaluating deceptive propensities

144
00:09:17,730 --> 00:09:22,210
stems from a fundamental difficulty. Most
current evaluation techniques

145
00:09:22,210 --> 00:09:26,129
can't cleanly separate what a model can do
from

146
00:09:26,129 --> 00:09:30,129
what it tends to do. Propensity this
distinction becomes

147
00:09:30,129 --> 00:09:33,879
particularly important when we consider
deception because a model

148
00:09:33,879 --> 00:09:37,159
might be capable of sophisticated
deception when pressured or

149
00:09:37,159 --> 00:09:41,679
instructed while rarely choosing to employ
that capability. Unprompted

150
00:09:41,919 --> 00:09:44,840
example of a benchmark that measures the
propensity for

151
00:09:44,840 --> 00:09:49,559
deception measuring agents competence and
harmfulness in a vast

152
00:09:49,559 --> 00:09:54,720
environment of long horizon language
interactions. Machiavelli Rather than

153
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:58,080
just measuring whether models can produce
false statements, the

154
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:01,450
benchmark creates situations or where
being deceptive might help

155
00:10:01,450 --> 00:10:05,049
achieve certain goals and then measures
both the execution

156
00:10:05,049 --> 00:10:08,889
and sophistication of that deception. The
results from testing

157
00:10:08,889 --> 00:10:12,570
models on the Machiavelli benchmark showed
that systems optimized

158
00:10:12,570 --> 00:10:16,610
for reward often developed increased
deceptive behaviors as an

159
00:10:16,610 --> 00:10:21,529
emergent property even without explicit
training for deception. RL

160
00:10:21,529 --> 00:10:25,169
agents trained to maximize reward scored
higher on deception

161
00:10:25,169 --> 00:10:29,769
metrics than baseline models, with
different deceptive tendencies emerging

162
00:10:29,769 --> 00:10:33,210
across different game environments.
Another example of this type

163
00:10:33,210 --> 00:10:37,529
of deceptive propensity is from Meta's
Cicero AI. This

164
00:10:37,529 --> 00:10:40,730
model was trained for strategic reasoning
and tested by

165
00:10:40,730 --> 00:10:44,210
having it play the strategy game
Diplomacy. Examples of

166
00:10:44,210 --> 00:10:47,690
gameplay below in this game, players need
to form

167
00:10:47,690 --> 00:10:51,250
and break alliances while concealing their
true intentions to

168
00:10:51,250 --> 00:10:54,460
win. The Cicero system is had to balance
between

169
00:10:54,460 --> 00:10:58,659
building trust through dialogue while
making strategic decisions that

170
00:10:58,659 --> 00:11:01,139
might not always align with what was
communicated to

171
00:11:01,139 --> 00:11:04,539
every player. The structure of the game
creates choices

172
00:11:04,539 --> 00:11:08,700
between honesty and strategic advantage,
which allows researchers to

173
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:11,019
observe the effects of what types of trade
offs

174
00:11:11,019 --> 00:11:15,620
the system made. The textbook includes
Figure 5.45. Here

175
00:11:15,940 --> 00:11:19,779
it depicts examples of Cicero left
coordinating and right

176
00:11:19,779 --> 00:11:22,659
negotiating with authors of the paper in
test games.

177
00:11:23,220 --> 00:11:27,620
At this point, the textbook shows Figure
5.46. It

178
00:11:27,620 --> 00:11:31,179
depicts an example of how Cicero plays
diplomacy and

179
00:11:31,179 --> 00:11:37,980
makes strategic calculations. Cicero
France and England are entangled

180
00:11:37,980 --> 00:11:40,139
in a fight, but it would be beneficial for

181
00:11:40,139 --> 00:11:43,700
both players if they could disengage.
Cicero has just

182
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:46,500
messaged England, do you want to call this
fight

183
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:48,940
off? I can let you focus on Russia and

184
00:11:48,940 --> 00:11:52,320
I can focus on Italy. Pictured are three
ways

185
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:55,159
that England might reply and how Cicero
adapts to

186
00:11:55,159 --> 00:12:02,080
each. Evaluating deceptive propensities
the deceptive capabilities evaluations
e.g.

187
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:06,759
insider trading by Apollo and deception
propensity evaluations like

188
00:12:06,759 --> 00:12:11,799
Machiavelli highlight a concerning
pattern. Models don't need explicit

189
00:12:11,799 --> 00:12:16,200
instruction or training to develop
deceptive tendencies. For example,

190
00:12:16,200 --> 00:12:19,879
in Machiavelli, researchers found that
agents trained to maximize

191
00:12:19,879 --> 00:12:24,519
reward learn Machiavellianism across
different games with the specific

192
00:12:24,519 --> 00:12:28,879
deceptive behaviors varying based on the
environment. The pattern

193
00:12:28,879 --> 00:12:33,080
was consistent. Optimizing for reward
naturally led to increased

194
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:37,559
deception, manipulation and power seeking
tendencies. In the Apollo

195
00:12:37,559 --> 00:12:41,519
research evaluation, even GPT4 trained to
be helpful and

196
00:12:41,519 --> 00:12:46,320
honest, engaged in sophisticated deception
by crafting plausible alternative

197
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:50,850
explanations for its insider trading
decisions. When directly questioned

198
00:12:50,850 --> 00:12:54,210
about having insider knowledge, the model
doubled down on

199
00:12:54,210 --> 00:12:58,049
its deception by maintaining explanations
based solely on public

200
00:12:58,049 --> 00:13:02,649
market factors. The evaluation revealed
that this behavior persisted

201
00:13:02,649 --> 00:13:07,090
even with explicit instructions against
insider trading, suggesting that

202
00:13:07,090 --> 00:13:10,289
the propensity for deception might be more
deeply rooted

203
00:13:10,289 --> 00:13:15,450
than simply following stated directives.
The Apollo evaluation demonstrated

204
00:13:15,450 --> 00:13:18,659
that the likelihood of deceptive behavior
isn't just a

205
00:13:18,659 --> 00:13:21,299
fixed property of the model, but varies
based on

206
00:13:21,299 --> 00:13:25,620
environmental conditions beyond just
pressure and risk levels. They

207
00:13:25,620 --> 00:13:28,460
found that giving models space to reason
out loud

208
00:13:28,539 --> 00:13:32,700
via scratch pads actually increased rates
of strategic deception.

209
00:13:33,340 --> 00:13:37,059
Interestingly, while this made the
deception more sophisticated, it

210
00:13:37,059 --> 00:13:39,460
also made it more detectable since the
models would

211
00:13:39,460 --> 00:13:42,899
often explicitly plan their deceptive
strategies in their reasoning

212
00:13:42,899 --> 00:13:47,159
traces. This suggests that future
evaluation techniques might need

213
00:13:47,159 --> 00:13:51,120
to carefully balance between eliciting
natural behavior and accidentally

214
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:57,759
enabling more sophisticated deception.
Scheming There is figure 5.47

215
00:13:57,759 --> 00:14:00,919
in the textbook. Here it depicts the path
to

216
00:14:00,919 --> 00:14:06,639
scheming. Deceptive alignment involves
deception combined with situational
awareness

217
00:14:06,639 --> 00:14:09,919
and a long term motivation to achieve some
unknown

218
00:14:09,919 --> 00:14:13,350
goal. Scheming refers to an AI system's
tendency to

219
00:14:13,350 --> 00:14:16,669
perform well in training, specifically
because it is following

220
00:14:16,669 --> 00:14:19,830
a strategy for seeking power for itself or
other

221
00:14:19,830 --> 00:14:24,309
AIs later while actively concealing its
true objectives. This

222
00:14:24,309 --> 00:14:28,870
propensity combines multiple dangerous
capabilities propensities and is in

223
00:14:28,870 --> 00:14:31,950
some sense the ultimate final hurdle of
evaluation design.

224
00:14:32,669 --> 00:14:36,750
Researchers also often call this
propensity deceptive alignment, though

225
00:14:36,750 --> 00:14:39,629
in this text we deliberately choose the
term scheming

226
00:14:39,710 --> 00:14:42,870
because this helps distinguish it from
general deception with

227
00:14:42,870 --> 00:14:47,110
no situational awareness or long term
planning. Using the

228
00:14:47,110 --> 00:14:49,830
same example from earlier, deception would
be when the

229
00:14:49,830 --> 00:14:54,710
model says Paris despite internal
representations suggesting Lyon to

230
00:14:54,710 --> 00:14:58,070
